[STUART.Description]
\t Sergeant Alan Mitchell enrolled into the US Army in quite an odd fashion: he lost his parents who died in a train wreck at his early age and he became an inmate of a private orphanage, whose board of trustees included the retired Colonel Smith. Becoming an adult, he met the Colonel’s daughter at one of the events held at this institution. The young couple fell in love, but the old war dog said that the marriage would only be possible, if Alan became a lieutenant and excelled in service. Alan Mitchell had no choice but to enroll voluntarily. But his service was far from being smooth...

\t An extract from the personal records of Alan Mitchell, the US Army sergeant:

\t...he was born in 1920, volunteered for the Army in June 1941, passed his training for a tank commander at Camp Beauregard. Family status: single, engaged to Nelly Smith, daughter of Colonel Graham W. Smith, retired (Patton’s comrade-in-arms during the 1917 French campaign, an acquaintance of Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters). He received no higher education, but repeatedly stated his wish to enter the university after completing his service term. He is keen on machinery, shows brilliant knowledge of material equipment and good tactics skills. He is unsociable with his comrades-in-arms and had several conflicts with the superior command, and as a result, he repeatedly received disciplinary punishments. However, he almost always completed the assigned tasks during maneuvers, though by unconventional methods. Commissioning as officer is recommended only in case of an earnest change of behavior towards the superior command.

[STUART.FullName]
Commander of a Stuart

[STUART.Epilogue]
Alan Mitchell was severely wounded not long before the capitulation of the Italian and German forces in Tunisia. After recovery, he was rejected for combatant service; he was recalled by Roger Waters, being promoted to Lieutenant, and held a small position at one of the US Army technical committees, where his combat experience and machinery mastery saw a proper use. In 1945, Alan Mitchell retired from military service. He started attending the university. In the end, the condition put forth by the old Colonel Smith was met and the family life of Alan and Nelly was as happy as unremarkable. Alan Mitchell died in 1984.

“The trade of war is simple and quite intelligible for a reasonable mind. But carrying a war is not easy.” Carl von Clausewitz.

[Clearing.Description]
\t Sergeant Alan Mitchell enrolled into the US Army in quite an odd fashion: he lost his parents who died in a train wreck at his early age and he became an inmate of a private orphanage, whose board of trustees included the retired Colonel Smith. Becoming an adult, he met the Colonel’s daughter at one of the events held at this institution. The young couple fell in love, but the old war dog said that the marriage would only be possible, if Alan became a lieutenant and excelled in service. Alan Mitchell had no choice but to enroll voluntarily. But his service was far from being smooth...

\t An extract from the personal records of Alan Mitchell, the US Army sergeant:

\t... he was born in 1920, volunteered for the Army in June 1941, passed his training for a tank commander at Camp Beauregard. Family status: single, engaged to Nelly Smith, daughter of Colonel Graham W. Smith, retired (Patton’s comrade-in-arms during the 1917 French campaign, an acquaintance of Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters). He received no higher education, but repeatedly stated his wish to enter the university after completing his service term. He is keen on machinery, shows brilliant knowledge of material equipment and good tactics skills. He is unsociable with his comrades-in-arms and had several conflicts with the superior command, and as a result, he repeatedly received disciplinary punishments. However, he almost always completed the assigned tasks during maneuvers, though by unconventional methods. Commissioning as officer is recommended only in case of an earnest change of behavior towards the superior command.

\t On 12 February 1943, Mitchell’s unit received an order to attack a village that was the fortified point of a German defensive line. It was the first real battle for Alan.

\t (To advance further with the campaign, keep Alan Mitchell alive, and his tank in working order).

[Clearing.FullName]
Clean-up operation

[StopTheGap.FullName]
Stop the Gap
[StopTheGap.Description]
\t Though Alan Mitchell received an award for the successful capture of the fortified point, his views on the service, command and war were very skeptical. This skepticism grew even stronger after Captain Jackson’s promotion, but after the tank crewman met Lieutenant Colonel Waters, many of his opinions changed. Despite the age gap and the rank gap, which was even bigger, Waters managed to earn Alan's trust and explain what he and other Americans were fighting for here, in Tunisia, that was so far from their country and home. In his turn, Waters noticed Mitchell's potential and ordered to transfer him to a light tank scouting company that was in a special need of competent and enterprising soldiers.

\t From Alan Mitchell’s letter to his fiancée:

\t "...now, when I’m far from you and my home, I see all things in a different way, both sharp and blurry at the same time. But now I’m confident and calm, if one can be confident and calm in this situation at all. Since I’ve been transferred to a reconnaissance company, this feeling that I’m some sort of a bull-calf at butcher Nouks’ slaughterhouse on Washington Street disappeared. And that is with the fact that the chances to go to heaven here, in a reconnaissance company, are higher than usual, to put it mildly. But I hate to say that I need this feeling, because Lieutenant Colonel Waters told me much about the Germans and their "new order", it’s something I would not want for our future. Anyway, why would they care about the way we live, or the English, the French or the Russians live? And I think I can trust Waters..."

\t On 19 February 1943, the Germans went on to the offensive and crushed the inexperienced American units defending the Kasserine pass. Their mobile groups began to follow up and destroy the isolated remnants of the retreating units; it was already a full-scale defeat and was about to become a total failure. The next day, on 20 February, a small tank detachment of the reconnaissance company was given an order to organize an ambush on the route of one of the presumptuous enemy mobile groups. That unit included Alan Mitchell.  

\t (To advance further with the campaign, Alan Mitchell must stay alive, and his tank must not be destroyed completely).

[ForTheLastSoldier.Description]
\t The defeat of the German mobile group cost dearly. All that was left from the tank group was Alan Mitchell's Stuart, and it took the whole night of 20 and 21 February to repair it. When all the minor damages were repaired, the completely worn out crew found itself deep in the rear of the advancing enemy forces. With the Stuart moving carefully towards their friendly forces, it suddenly came across a depleted American infantry company that organized a defense in several small villages close to each other. Its courageous but inexperienced commander decided to fight to the very last soldier with his thinly stretched forces. But the enemy, knowing of the weakness of the defendants, did not assign major forces to destroy them, so even one Stuart could really change the course of events. After receiving Waters’ approval to cover the company until reinforcements could arrive, Alan Mitchell began preparing his tank and crew for a tough battle.   

\t The events were developing so rapidly that he had no time to read Nelly’s letter. But during a short hush before the fight he opened the envelope with a field post address written by her hand, but there was a surprise inside. In the envelope there was a piece of paper with some shaky old man’s handwriting:
\t "Mitchell, you bastard! Don’t you ever try to write anything like this ever again! After this babbler Stanley got his article for County Chronicle, all neighborhood honors you as a hero, and now you're gonna die for some lofty matters. Don’t you dare to think of anything like this until you’re at least a Colonel, you goddamn philosopher! If you, son of a bitch, get killed, my daughter will surely lay hands on herself, but it won't save you or her - I'm gonna find you beyond the veil and make Saint Peter give you a good thrashing! So, you gotta return an officer, and forget about that asshole Jackson - this sucker's gonna get what he deserves!"

\t Apparently, old Colonel Smith reads all his correspondence, too, and now he replaced the letters! Remembering that iron discipline and obedience that prevailed in his house, Alan smiled, put the letter into his board and went to his Stuart...

\t (To complete the campaign successfully, Alan Mitchell must stay alive, and his tank must not be damaged).
[ForTheLastSoldier.FullName]
For The Last Soldier
